\v 32 So¦10310 \add Abraham's slave\add* went¦10310 to¦10312 the¦10312 house¦10312. Then¦10310 he¦10313 unloaded¦10313 the¦10314 camels¦10314 and¦10315 gave¦10315 them straw¦10316 and¦10317 fodder¦10317, and¦10319 water¦10319 to¦10320 wash¦10320 his¦10321 feet and¦10322 the¦10322 feet¦10322 of¦10322 the¦10323 men¦10323 who¦10324 were with¦10325 him¦10325.\f + \fr 24:32 \ft It's not certain from the pronouns in the text here, just who did what in this second sentence—whether it was the slave (the guest) or Lavan (the host), so other translations might vary.\f*
\v 33 Then¦10327 food was¦10327 set¦10327 out for \add them\add* to eat, but¦10327 \add Abraham's slave\add* said¦10337, “I won't¦10332 eat¦10333 until¦10334 I have¦10337 \add said what I have to say\add*.”
\p “Speak¦10340 then¦10327,” Lavan responded¦10331.
