\p
\v 22 Three days¦14713 later, Lavan¦14712 was¦14711 \add informed\add* that¦14715 Yacob¦14717 had¦14716 fled¦14716 \add with his family\add*,
\v 23 \add so\add* he¦14719 took¦14719 \add some of\add* his relatives¦14722 with¦14731 him¦14725 and chased¦14724 after¦14725 Yacob \add and his family\add* for seven¦14727 days¦14728 until they overtook¦14729 \add them\add* in¦14731 the¦14731 hill-country of¦14731 Gilead¦14732.
\v 24 \add But\add* that night¦14741 God¦14735 \add appeared\add* to¦14736 Lavan¦14738 the¦14739 Syrian¦14739 in¦14740 a dream¦14740 and¦14742 told¦14742 him¦14743, “Watch yourself¦14745 that you¦14748 don't \add say anything to\add* Yacob¦14751, either good¦14752 or bad¦14755.”
\v 25 \add When\add* Lavan had caught up to Yacob, Yacob had pitched¦14763 \add their\add* tents¦14766 in¦14767 the hills, so Lavan with¦14767 his¦14766 relatives¦14772 \add also\add* pitched theirs on¦14767 the Gilead¦14774 hills.
\p
\v 26 Then¦14776 Lavan¦14777 said¦14776 to¦14778 Yacob¦14778, “What¦14779 have¦14780 you¦14780 done¦14780? \add You've deceived me\add* and¦14776 carried¦14785 away¦14785 my¦14784 daughters¦14788 like¦14789 \add prisoners of war\add*.
\v 27 Why did¦14793 you¦14793 flee¦14794 secretly¦14793 and¦14795 deceive¦14795 me¦14796 and¦14797 not¦14797 tell¦14799 me¦14800? I¦14801 would have sent¦14801 you¦14801 \add all off\add* with¦14802 \add music and singing and a cheery party\add*.
\v 28 You¦14808 didn't¦14807 let¦14808 me¦14808 kiss¦14809 my¦14810 grandchildren \add goodbye\add* and¦14811 my daughters¦14811, \add instead you made a foolish decision\add*.
\v 29 \add I have the right and the ability to punish\add* you¦14821, but¦14823 last¦14825 night¦14825 your¦14824 father's¦14824 god¦14823 told¦14827 me¦14828, ‘\add Be careful not to\add* speak¦14832 either good¦14836 or bad¦14839 to Yacob¦14835.’
\v 30 \add So\add* now¦14841, you¦14843 probably left¦14843 because¦14844 you¦14847 miss your¦14849 father's¦14849 place badly, \add but\add* why did¦14851 you¦14851 steal¦14851 my¦14854 gods¦14854?”
\p
\v 31 “\add We left like that,\add*” Yacob¦14857 answered¦14856 Lavan¦14859, “because¦14860 I was¦14861 afraid¦14861, because¦14862 I said¦14863 that perhaps you¦14866 would \add use force to take\add* your¦14869 \add married\add* daughters¦14869 from¦14870 me¦14870.
\v 32 \add As for your idols,\add* anyone¦14873 you¦14874 find¦14874 your¦14877 gods¦14877 with will¦14879 \add be put to death\add*. Look through everything we have¦14896 and¦14887 take¦14887 \add back anything that\add* belongs to you, with our¦14881 relatives¦14881 \add as witnesses\add*.” (Now Yacob¦14893 didn't¦14878 know¦14892 that¦14894 Rahel¦14895 had¦14896 stolen¦14896 them¦14896.)
\p
\v 33 \add So\add* Lavan¦14899 \add inspected\add* Yacob's¦14901 and Le'ah's tents¦14900, and the tent of the two¦14906 slave¦14907 women, but¦14898 he¦14898 didn't¦14908 find¦14909 anything. Then¦14898 he came¦14910 out¦14910 of Le'ah's tent and¦14913 went¦14913 into¦14914 Rahel's¦14915 tent.
\v 34 Now Rahel¦14917 had¦14918 taken¦14918 the¦14921 idols¦14921 and¦14917 put¦14922 them¦14922 in¦14923 the camel's¦14924 saddle¦14923 \add which she was now\add* sitting¦14925 on¦14923, \add so although\add* Lavan¦14928 searched¦14927 throughout \add her\add* entire¦14931 tent¦14933, he¦14927 didn't¦14934 find¦14935 them¦14935,
\v 35 and¦14937 she¦14937 said¦14937 to¦14938 her¦14940 father¦14940, “Don't¦14941 let¦14943 my¦14945 master¦14945 \add be upset\add* that I'm¦14948 not¦14947 able¦14948 to stand up in¦14944 your¦14950 presence¦14950, because¦14946 \add I have the regular female concern at the moment\add*.” So¦14937 he¦14956 searched¦14956, but¦14937 he didn't¦14941 find¦14958 the¦14961 idols¦14961.
\p
\v 36 \add So\add* Yacob¦14964 \add got very angry\add* and¦14963 he¦14965 argued with¦14966 Lavan¦14966, demanding from \add him\add*, “What's¦14971 my¦14973 crime? What's¦14974 my sin¦14975, that you've¦14977 hotly¦14977 chased¦14977 after¦14978 me¦14978?
\v 37 Since you've¦14982 searched¦14982 through all¦14985 \add our\add* things¦14987, what¦14988 things from¦14991 \em your¦14994\em* house¦14994 have¦14982 you¦14990 found¦14990? Put¦14995 it here in front¦14997 of¦14998 my¦14998 relatives and your relatives, and let them judge between¦15001 the¦15002 two¦15002 of¦15002 us¦15002!
\p
\v 38 “I've¦15016 \add worked\add* for you¦15008 for twenty¦15005 years¦15006. Your¦15009 ewes¦15009 and¦15010 your female¦15010 goats¦15010 didn't¦15011 miscarry¦15012, and I¦15007 haven't eaten¦15016 rams¦15013 from your¦15014 flocks¦15014.
\v 39 I didn't¦15019 bring¦15021 \add dead sheep or goats\add* to¦15022 you¦15022 \add that had been attacked by wild animals\add*—I bore¦15024 the¦15024 loss¦15024 of¦15024 \add those\add* myself. You¦15026 required¦15026 \add that I cover anything\add* stolen¦15027 during the day¦15028 or during the night¦15030.
\v 40 I was¦15032 \add always out there\add*—during¦15033 the day¦15033 the heat¦15035 \add tormented\add* me¦15034, and¦15036 at¦15033 night¦15037 the frost¦15036 \add so bad that I couldn't sleep\add*.
\v 41 That's how it was for¦15044 me¦15044 for twenty¦15045 years in¦15047 your house¦15047. I¦15048 \add worked for you for\add* fourteen years for¦15053 your two¦15053 daughters¦15054, then six¦15055 \add more\add* years¦15056 for¦15057 your flocks¦15057, and¦15058 you¦15058 changed¦15058 my¦15061 wages¦15061 ten¦15062 times¦15063 \add to your advantage\add*.
\v 42 If¦15074 my father's¦15067 god—the¦15068 god¦15068 of¦15068 Abraham¦15069 and¦15070 the¦15070 one Yitshak¦15071 respected¦15070—had¦15072 not¦15065 been¦15072 with me¦15073, surely you¦15077 would have sent¦15077 me¦15077 away¦15077 empty-handed¦15076 now¦15075. God¦15066 has¦15086 seen¦15086 my suffering and \add my hard work\add*, and last¦15089 night¦15089, he¦15088 rebuked¦15088 you.”
\s1 Yacob and Lavan make a treaty
\rem /s1 Jacob's Treaty with Laban; Laban and Jacob Make a Covenant; The Agreement between Jacob and Laban
\p
\v 43 Then¦15091 Lavan¦15092 responded¦15093 and told¦15093 Yacob¦15096, “\add These women\add* are¦15098 my¦15098 daughters, and¦15099 the¦15099 children are¦15100 my¦15100 grandchildren, and¦15101 the¦15101 flocks are¦15102 my¦15102 flocks. Everything¦15103 that¦15104 you¦15106 see¦15107 \add here\add* belongs to¦15108 me¦15108. But¦15091 what¦15112 can I¦15114 do¦15114 today about these¦15115 daughters of¦15111 mine or¦15117 about their¦15118 children that they¦15120 gave birth¦15120 to?
\v 44 \add So\add* come¦15123 now¦15122, let's¦15124 make¦15124 an agreement¦15125, I¦15126 and you, and let¦15124 it¦15128 be¦15128 a witness¦15129 between¦15130 me¦15130 and¦15131 you¦15131.”
\p
\v 45 So¦15133 Yacob¦15134 took¦15133 a stone¦15135 and¦15133 \add stood it up longways\add* as a pillar¦15137,
\v 46 then \add he instructed\add* his¦15141 relatives¦15141, “Gather¦15140 stones.” So¦15139 they \add fetched\add* stones and¦15139 made¦15146 a¦15148 pile, then they¦15149 ate¦15149 there¦15150 by the¦15153 pile.
\v 47 Lavan¦15158 gave the pile \add the Aramaic name\add* ‘Yegar¦15159 Sahadutha¦15160’ \add (which means ‘pile that reminds’)\add* while Yacob¦15161 gave it¦15155 \add the Hebrew name\add* ‘Gale'ed’ \add (which has the same meaning)\add*.
\p
\v 48 Then¦15166 Lavan¦15167 said¦15166, “This¦15169 pile¦15168 is¦15170 a witness¦15170 \add of the agreement\add* between¦15171 me¦15171 and¦15166 you¦15172 today.” (That's why he'd named¦15179 it¦15166 Galeed¦15181.)
\v 49 It's also¦15189 \add named\add* ‘Mitspah¦15183’ \add (which means ‘watchtower’)\add*, because¦15190 he¦15185 said¦15185, “May¦15186 Yahweh¦15187 watch¦15186 between¦15188 me¦15188 and¦15189 you¦15189 when we¦15191 are¦15191 hidden¦15191 one from¦15193 the¦15183 other¦15193.
\v 50 If¦15198 you \add mistreat\add* my daughters, or if¦15201 you¦15203 take¦15203 \add other\add* wives¦15204 besides my daughters, even though there's¦15208 not¦15208 someone¦15209 \add there to see it\add*, listen, God¦15212 \add will be\add* a witness¦15213 between¦15214 me¦15214 and¦15215 you¦15215.”
\v 51 Then¦15217 Lavan¦15218 said¦15217 to¦15219 Yacob¦15219, “Look at this¦15223 pile¦15222 \add of stones\add*, and¦15224 look at the¦15225 pillar¦15225 which¦15227 I¦15228 set¦15228 up¦15228 between¦15229 me¦15229 and¦15230 you¦15230.
\v 52 This¦15242 pile is a witness and the¦15236 pillar¦15236 is a witness that I¦15239 won't go past¦15242 these to¦15243 you¦15243, and¦15248 that you won't go past¦15253 these to¦15254 me¦15254 to do harm¦15263.
\v 53 May¦15269 Abraham's¦15266 god¦15265 and¦15267 the¦15267 gods of¦15267 their¦15272 ancestor Nahor¦15268 judge¦15269 between¦15270 us¦15270.” Then Yacob¦15274 swore¦15273 by¦15275 the¦15275 one respected¦15275 by his¦15276 father¦15276 Yitshak¦15277,
\v 54 and¦15279 Yacob¦15280 offered a sacrifice¦15279 on the hill. Then¦15279 he¦15283 called¦15283 his¦15284 relatives¦15284 to¦15285 eat¦15285 bread and¦15288 they¦15288 ate¦15288 bread and¦15290 spent¦15290 the¦15290 night¦15290 on the hill.
\v 55 In the morning, Lavan got up early and \add gave\add* his grandchildren and his daughters \add a kiss\add*, and he blessed them. Then \add he\add* left and returned to his place.
\c 32
\s1 Yacob encounters God's messengers
