\v 17 “In¦19984 my¦19984 dream¦19984,” Far'oh¦19980 told¦19979 Yosef¦19983, “wow¦19985, I¦19985 was¦19986 standing¦19986 on¦19984 the¦19989 river¦19990 bank¦19989
\v 18 and¦19992 then¦19992 listen, up¦19996 from¦19993 the¦19995 river¦19995 came¦19996 seven¦19997 cows¦19998. \add They were\add* well-fed and¦20001 good-looking, and¦20003 they¦20003 grazed¦20003 among¦20004 the reeds¦20004.
\v 19 Then¦20006 listen, seven¦20007 other cows¦20009 came¦20011 up¦20011 after¦20012 them¦20012. \add They were in\add* poor \add condition\add* and¦20014 very¦20016 ugly and¦20017 skinny—I¦20021 haven't seen¦20021 cows that ugly \add anywhere in\add* Egypt¦20026.
\v 20 Then¦20033 the skinny, ugly cows ate¦20029 the first¦20036 seven¦20034 fat¦20037 cows,
\v 21 \add but\add* you wouldn't know¦20044 it¦20044 because¦20045 their¦20042 bellies \add were just as skinny as before\add* and¦20039 they¦20039 looked just as ugly as they¦20047 had¦20047 before. Then¦20039 I¦20055 woke¦20055 up.
\p
\v 22 “Then¦20057 I \add had another\add* dream¦20058, and¦20059 listen, seven¦20061 heads¦20062 of¦20062 grain¦20062 came¦20063 up¦20063 on¦20058 one¦20065 stalk¦20064. \add They were\add* full¦20066 and¦20067 good¦20067.
\v 23 Then¦20069 listen, seven¦20070 \add more\add* heads¦20071 of¦20071 grain¦20071 sprouted after¦20077 them¦20077, \add but they were\add* withered¦20072 and thin¦20073, and scorched¦20074 by the east¦20075 wind¦20075.
\v 24 Then¦20079 the thin¦20082 heads of grain swallowed¦20079 up the seven¦20084 good¦20086 heads of grain. I¦20083 told¦20092 \add these dreams\add* to the sorcerers, but¦20079 no one¦20092 could explain \add them\add* to¦20093 me¦20093.”
\p
\v 25 Then¦20095 Yosef¦20096 said¦20095 to¦20097 Far'oh¦20099, “\add The king's\add* dreams¦20100 are¦20102 one¦20102 \add and the same\add*. God¦20106 has¦20108 shown Far'oh what he's¦20108 going to do¦20107.
\v 26 The¦20113 seven good cows¦20112 are¦20114 seven¦20114 years¦20115, and¦20117 the seven good heads¦20118 of grain¦20118 are¦20120 seven¦20120 years¦20121—\add both\add* dreams¦20123 \add mean the same\add*.
\v 27 And¦20127 the seven thin and ugly cows¦20128 that¦20131 came¦20131 up¦20131 after¦20132 them¦20132 are¦20133 seven¦20133 years¦20134, and¦20136 so are the seven thin heads¦20137 of grain¦20137 scorched¦20139 by the¦20140 east¦20140 wind¦20140. There will¦20141 be¦20141 seven¦20142 years¦20143 of¦20143 \add drought\add*.
\v 28 That's¦20153 \add why\add* I¦20149 told¦20149 Far'oh¦20152 that¦20148 God¦20154 has¦20156 shown¦20156 \add him\add* what he's¦20156 going to¦20150 do¦20155.
\v 29 Listen, there's¦20161 seven¦20162 years¦20163 coming¦20164 of plentiful¦20165 \add harvests\add* throughout all¦20167 of¦20169 Egypt¦20170,
\v 30 \add but\add* seven¦20173 years¦20175 of¦20175 \add drought\add* will \add follow\add* after¦20177 them¦20177, \add such that the years of plenty will be quickly\add* forgotten¦20178 and¦20172 famine¦20185 will \add devastate\add* the¦20188 land¦20188.
\v 31 \add Yes,\add* the \add time of plenty\add* won't¦20190 be¦20192 remembered because¦20201 of the famine¦20196 \add that follows\add*—it'll¦20192 be¦20203 very¦20205 \add severe\add*.
\v 32 And¦20207 as for \add having two dreams with the same meaning\add*, it's¦20222 because¦20214 the¦20217 matter¦20217 has been established by God, and¦20220 God will do¦20222 it¦20222 soon.
\p
\v 33 “So¦20224 now¦20224 \add the king\add* should¦20225 find a man¦20227 who's discerning¦20228 and¦20229 wise¦20229, and appoint him¦20225 over¦20231 \add all\add* Egypt¦20234.
\v 34 Then \add the king\add* should¦20238 get¦20243 him¦20236 to appoint¦20238 supervisors¦20239 \add all across the country\add* to collect twenty¦20243 percent of¦20243 the¦20246 \add crops\add* during¦20248 the seven¦20248 years¦20249 of¦20249 plenty¦20250.
\v 35 They¦20271 should collect¦20252 that¦20260 \add surplus\add* food¦20257 in the coming¦20260 good¦20259 years¦20258, and¦20252 store¦20262 it in the cities¦20270 \add under the king's authority\add*. It¦20268 should be¦20260 kept¦20271
\v 36 and¦20273 used as a reserve¦20275 for¦20276 the seven¦20277 years¦20278 of¦20278 famine¦20279 that¦20280 will affect the¦20287 country¦20287, so that the \add people\add* will survive.
\s1 Yosef becomes a ruler in Egypt
\rem /s1 Joseph's Rise to Power; Joseph Is Made Governor over Egypt; Joseph Made Ruler of Egypt
\p
\v 37 \add This idea sounded\add* good¦20290 to Far'oh¦20293 and¦20294 to all¦20295 his¦20297 servants¦20297,
\v 38 so he¦20299 asked¦20299 them, “\add Do we have any other\add* man¦20306 like¦20305 this¦20305 who has¦20304 God's¦20309 spirit¦20308 in¦20310 him¦20310?”
\v 39 Then¦20312 he¦20312 told¦20312 Yosef¦20316, “Since¦20313 God¦20319 has¦20318 shown you¦20320 all¦20323 this¦20325, there's¦20326 no one as discerning¦20328 and¦20312 wise¦20329 as¦20330 you¦20330.
\v 40 You'll be¦20333 over¦20334 my household¦20336, and¦20337 all¦20341 my people¦20343 will \add obey your every word\add*. Only¦20344 I¦20346 on¦20337 the¦20345 throne will¦20346 be¦20346 greater¦20346 than¦20347 you¦20347.”\x + \xo 41:40: \xt Acts 7:10.\x*
\rem /s1 Joseph in Charge of Egypt
\v 41 Then¦20349 \add he added\add*, “See¦20354, I've put¦20355 you¦20356 over¦20357 all¦20358 the¦20360 \add entire country\add*.”
\v 42 Then¦20363 \add he\add* removed¦20363 his signet¦20367 ring from¦20368 his¦20369 hand¦20369 and¦20370 put it¦20370 on¦20372 Yosef's¦20375 hand, and¦20376 he¦20376 had him¦20377 dressed¦20376 in fine¦20380 linen¦20380 clothes¦20376, and¦20381 put a gold¦20383 chain¦20382 around his neck¦20386.\x + \xo 41:42: \xt Dan 5:29.\x*
\v 43 He¦20388 had \add Yosef\add* ride¦20388 in¦20390 his¦20396 #2 chariot¦20390, and¦20388 they¦20395 called out in front¦20396 of him, “Bow down!” So¦20388 \add the king\add* put¦20398 him¦20399 over¦20400 the entire¦20401 \add country\add* of Egypt¦20404.
\v 44 Far'oh \add also\add* told¦20406 \add him\add*, “I'm¦20411 Far'oh, and¦20406 no person in¦20424 all¦20424 Egypt¦20427 will¦20416 lift¦20416 their hand¦20420 or foot¦20423 without¦20413 \add your permission\add*.”
\v 45 Then¦20429 Far'oh¦20430 gave¦20436 him¦20438 the Egyptian name¦20431 ‘Zafenath¦20434-Paneah¦20435’ \add (which means ‘the one who reveals secrets’)\add*, and¦20429 he¦20436 \add presented him with\add* Asenath¦20441, the¦20442 daughter¦20442 of¦20442 Potifera the¦20446 priest¦20446 of¦20446 On¦20447, as a¦20448 wife¦20448. And¦20449 Yosef¦20433 \add travelled throughout\add* the¦20453 land¦20453 of¦20453 Egypt¦20454.
\p
\v 46 Yosef¦20456 was¦20457 thirty¦20459 years¦20460 old when¦20461 he'd \add been called to\add* stand¦20461 before¦20462 Far'oh¦20470 king¦20464 of Egypt. \add When he left\add* Far'oh's¦20463 presence¦20469, he¦20471 travelled¦20471 throughout the¦20475 land¦20475 of¦20475 Egypt
\v 47 and¦20478 the¦20479 land¦20479 produced¦20478 outstanding¦20483 harvests¦20483 during the seven¦20480 years¦20481 of¦20481 plenty¦20482.
\v 48 For seven¦20492 years¦20493, he gathered¦20485 all¦20488 the food¦20490 throughout the¦20493 land¦20496 of¦20497 Egypt¦20497 and¦20485 had it¦20507 stored¦20498 in¦20501 the cities¦20501. The¦20490 food from the countryside¦20503 that¦20506 surrounded¦20507 each¦20488 city¦20505, he¦20508 put¦20508 inside¦20509 \add that city\add*.
\v 49 \add So\add* Yosef¦20512 stored¦20511 up¦20511 so much grain¦20513 \add that it was\add* like¦20514 sand¦20514 \add on the beaches\add*, until¦20518 he¦20521 \add eventually\add* stopped¦20521 measuring¦20522 it because¦20519 it \add too much to calculate\add*.
\p
\v 50 Before¦20532 the¦20534 years¦20534 of¦20534 the¦20535 famine¦20535 came¦20533, Yosef¦20528 had two¦20530 sons¦20531 that Asenath¦20540 (the¦20541 daughter¦20541 of¦20541 Potifera, the¦20545 priest¦20545 of¦20545 On¦20546) gave birth¦20537 to¦20528.
\v 51 Yosef¦20549 named¦20552 the¦20552 oldest¦20553 son ‘Manasseh¦20554’ \add (which means ‘forget’)\add*, because¦20555 he¦20557 said, “God¦20558 has¦20557 made¦20557 me¦20557 forget¦20557 all¦20561 my hardships¦20563 and¦20548 all¦20565 of¦20565 my family¦20567.”
\v 52 His second¦20572 son he named¦20571 ‘Efrayim¦20574’ \add (which means ‘fruitful’)\add*, because¦20575 he¦20577 said, “God¦20578 has¦20577 made¦20577 me¦20577 fruitful¦20577 in¦20579 the¦20571 land¦20579 of¦20580 my¦20580 difficulties.”
\p
\v 53 Then¦20582 the¦20584 seven¦20583 years¦20584 of¦20584 plenty¦20585 in¦20588 Egypt¦20589 came¦20582 to¦20582 an¦20582 end¦20582
\v 54 and¦20591 the seven¦20592 years¦20593 of¦20593 famine started¦20591, just¦20596 as¦20596 Yosef¦20598 had¦20597 said¦20597. The¦20594 famine \add covered the entire wider region\add*, but¦20591 \add it was different in\add* Egypt¦20607.\x + \xo 41:54: \xt Acts 7:11.\x*
\v 55 All¦20612 the¦20614 land¦20614 of¦20614 Egypt¦20615 became famished¦20611, and¦20616 the¦20617 people¦20617 cried¦20616 to¦20618 \add the king\add* for¦20621 food¦20621, so Far'oh¦20620 said¦20622 to¦20624 all¦20624 the Egyptians¦20626, “Go¦20627 to¦20628 Yosef¦20630 \add and\add* do¦20635 whatever he¦20633 tells¦20633 you.”\x + \xo 41:55: \xt Yhn 2:5.\x*
\v 56 The¦20637 famine¦20637 was¦20638 \add right across the country\add*, so Yosef¦20645 opened¦20644 all¦20648 the grain stores and¦20637 sold grain¦20652 to¦20653 the Egyptians. \add But\add* the famine¦20655 in¦20656 Egypt¦20657 was \add really\add* severe¦20654.
\v 57 Then¦20659 \add people from much further away started\add* coming¦20662 to¦20663 Egypt¦20663 to¦20664 buy¦20664 grain¦20664 from Yosef¦20667, because¦20668 the¦20671 famine¦20671 was¦20670 severe¦20670 in¦20672 the¦20674 \add entire wider region\add*.
\c 42
\s1 Yosef's brothers turn up in Egypt
\rem /s1 Joseph's Brothers Go to Egypt to Buy Grain; Joseph's Brothers Go to Egypt
\p
\v 1 \add Meanwhile (up in the Canaan region),\add* Yacob¦20677 \add heard\add* that¦20678 there¦20679 was¦20679 grain¦20681 \add available\add* in¦20682 Egypt¦20682, so \add he\add* said¦20683 to¦20685 his¦20685 sons¦20685, “Why are¦20687 you¦20687 \add just standing there\add* looking¦20687 at¦20682 each other?
\v 2 Listen¦20690, I've¦20691 heard¦20691 that¦20692 \add they're selling\add* grain¦20695 in¦20696 Egypt¦20696. Go¦20697 down¦20697 there¦20693 and¦20689 buy¦20700 some for¦20702 us¦20702 so¦20704 that¦20704 we¦20706 \add won't just starve to death here\add*.”\x + \xo 42:2: \xt Acts 7:12.\x*
\v 3 \add So\add* ten¦20712 of¦20709 Yosef's¦20711 brothers¦20709 \add travelled\add* down¦20708 to¦20713 Egypt¦20715 to buy¦20713 grain¦20714,
\v 4 \add but\add* Yacob¦20725 wouldn't send¦20724 Benyamin¦20719 (Yosef's¦20721 \add youngest\add* brother¦20720) with¦20726 \add the others\add*, because¦20729 he¦20724 said¦20730, “I don't¦20722 want any harm¦20734 to come¦20733 to¦20733 him¦20733.”
\p
\v 5 \add So\add* Yisra'el's¦20738 sons¦20737 \add found themselves\add* among \add many others\add* who¦20741 \add went\add* to¦20739 buy¦20739 grain¦20739, because¦20742 the famine¦20745 was¦20744 \add also throughout\add* the Canaan¦20747 \add region\add*.
\v 6 \add Now\add* Yosef¦20749 was¦20751 the¦20751 governor¦20751 over¦20752 \add all of Egypt\add* and he¦20755 \add was the one there\add* who¦20756 was selling¦20756 grain¦20756 to¦20757 all¦20757 the¦20759 people¦20759. So¦20749 Yosef's¦20763 brothers¦20762 \add approached\add* and¦20764 bowed¦20764 down¦20764 to¦20766 him¦20766 with their noses to the¦20768 ground¦20768.
\v 7 \add When\add* Yosef¦20771 saw¦20770 his¦20774 brothers¦20774, he¦20775 recognized¦20775 them¦20775 and¦20776 acted¦20776 like¦20776 a stranger¦20776 to¦20777 them¦20777, and¦20778 he¦20778 spoke¦20778 harshly¦20780 with¦20779 them \add (through an interpreter)\add*, “Where¦20783 \add are you lot from\add*?”
\p “From¦20783 the¦20786 land¦20786 of¦20786 Canaan¦20787 to¦20788 buy¦20788 food¦20790,” they¦20785 \add ≈answered¦20781\add*.
\p
\v 8 Yosef¦20793 had recognised¦20792 his¦20796 brothers¦20796, but¦20792 they¦20797 hadn't recognised¦20799 him¦20799,
\v 9 and¦20801 \add just then\add* he¦20801 remembered¦20801 the¦20804 dreams¦20804 that¦20805 he¦20806 had¦20806 dreamed¦20806 about¦20815 them, \add so\add* he said¦20808 to¦20809 them¦20809, “You are¦20810 spies¦20810. You've \add all\add* come¦20817 to \add discover any weak points in this country's defences\add*.”\x + \xo 42:9: \xt Gen 37:5-10.\x*
\p
\v 10 “No¦20821, my¦20822 master¦20822,” they¦20819 \add ≈answered¦20819\add*. “Your¦20823 servants¦20823 have¦20824 \add only\add* come¦20824 to¦20825 buy¦20825 food¦20827.
\v 11 We're \add all\add* sons¦20830 of¦20830 \add the same\add* man¦20831. We're \add being\add* honest¦20835. Your¦20840 servants¦20840 are¦20839 not¦20837 spies¦20841.”
\p
\v 12 “No¦20845, you've definitely \add all\add* come¦20850 \add here\add* to¦20851 see¦20851 \add where our land is vulnerable\add*,” \add Yosef insisted\add*.
\p
\v 13 “Your¦20856 servants¦20856 are¦20854 twelve brothers¦20857,” they¦20853 protested. “We're \add all\add* sons¦20860 of¦20860 one man¦20861 in¦20864 the Canaan¦20865 \add region\add*. \add It's all true\add*—the¦20867 youngest is¦20868 \add home\add* with¦20868 our¦20870 father¦20870, and¦20853 one \add has passed away\add*.”
\p
\v 14 “\add Yes,\add* it's¦20878 just as I¦20880 said¦20875,” retorted Yosef¦20877. “You're spies¦20883!
\v 15 \add But\add* this¦20886 is¦20887 how we'll test¦20887 you all: On Far'oh's¦20889 life¦20888, you won't \add ever\add* leave¦20892 this place unless your youngest brother¦20898 comes¦20897 here¦20893.
\v 16 \add Select\add* one¦20904 among you to go and get¦20905 your¦20903 brother¦20908, and¦20909 \add the rest of\add* you will¦20921 be¦20911 \add imprisoned\add*. Then \add we'll find out if you've all been telling the truth\add*. And¦20915 if¦20915 not¦20917, on Far'oh's¦20919 life¦20918, you're certainly spies¦20921.”
\v 17 Then¦20925 he¦20924 had them¦20925 \add locked up\add* together¦20924 for three¦20929 days¦20930.
\p
\v 18 On the¦20935 third¦20936 day¦20935, Yosef¦20934 told¦20932 them¦20933, “I'm¦20943 a man who reveres God¦20942. So¦20932 if you do¦20938 \add exactly\add* what I say¦20932, I'll let you live¦20939.
\v 19 If¦20946 you are¦20948 \add actually\add* honest¦20948, leave¦20956 one¦20951 of¦20953 your brothers¦20950 \add in prison here\add*, and¦20955 you \add others\add* go¦20956 and take¦20957 grain¦20958 back \add to feed\add* your families¦20960.
\v 20 Then¦20962 \add when\add* you will¦20966 bring¦20966 me¦20967 your youngest brother¦20964 so¦20968 that¦20968 \add I can verify your statements\add*, then¦20962 you won't¦20970 die¦20971.” So¦20962 they¦20972 \add agreed\add*,
\v 21 saying¦20976 to each¦20977 other \add (in Hebrew)\add*, “We¦20984 are¦20982 truly guilty¦20982 concerning our¦20987 brother¦20987 \add Yosef\add*, because we saw¦20989 the¦20990 distress¦20990 of¦20987 his¦20980 soul¦20991 when¦20992 he¦20992 pleaded¦20992 with¦20992 us, but¦20976 we didn't¦20994 \add take any notice\add*. \add That's why\add* this¦21002 \add predicament has happened to\add* us.”
\p
\v 22 “Didn't¦21022 I¦21009 \add beg\add* you \add all\add* not¦21008 to sin¦21015 against¦21016 the lad?” Reuben¦21005 spoke up. “But¦21004 you wouldn't listen¦21018, so now, see, \add now we're having to account for\add* his¦21021 blood¦21021.”\x + \xo 42:22: \xt Gen 37:21-22.\x*
\v 23 (\add But\add* they¦21025 didn't¦21026 know¦21027 that¦21028 Yosef¦21030 understood them, because¦21028 \add he and them had been speaking through an interpreter\add*.)
\v 24 \add Yosef\add* turned away¦21035 from them \add briefly so they couldn't see his tears\add*, then¦21035 he¦21035 turned back toward them and spoke¦21040 to¦21041 them. Then¦21035 he had Simeon¦21046 \add separated\add* from \add the group\add* and¦21047 tied¦21047 up in front of¦21049 them.
\v 25 Then¦21051 Yosef¦21052 ordered \add his servants\add* and¦21053 they filled¦21053 their¦21056 bags with grain¦21057, and he¦21051 \add quietly\add* ordered them to¦21058 return¦21058 each¦21060 man's silver¦21059 \add back\add* to¦21061 his sack¦21063 and¦21064 to¦21064 give¦21064 them¦21065 provisions¦21066 to them for¦21067 the road.
\s1 Yosef's brothers return home
\rem /s1 Joseph's Brothers Return to Canaan
\p \add After Yosef had provided for his brothers\add*,
\v 26 they¦21072 loaded¦21072 their grain¦21075 onto their¦21075 donkeys¦21078 and¦21072 departed¦21079.
\v 27 At¦21095 a lodging¦21090 place¦21090 \add that night\add*, one¦21083 \add of them\add* opened¦21082 his sack to feed his donkey¦21089 and¦21082 \add to his horror\add*, he¦21091 saw¦21091 his silver¦21094 inside the top of the sack¦21099.
\v 28 “My¦21108 silver's¦21106 been¦21105 returned¦21105,” he¦21121 told¦21101 his¦21104 brothers¦21104, “and¦21101 it's¦21105 \add right here\add* in¦21109 my¦21109 sack¦21109!” Then¦21101 their¦21111 \add ≈stomachs tightened\add*, and trembling¦21112, they¦21112 \add ≈asked¦21101\add* each¦21113 other, “What's¦21118 this¦21120 that¦21120 God's¦21122 doing¦21121 to¦21123 us¦21123?”
\p
\v 29 \add When\add* they¦21125 \add got home\add* to¦21126 Yacob¦21128 their¦21129 father¦21129 in Canaan¦21131, they¦21132 told¦21132 him¦21133 everything that¦21137 had¦21137 happened¦21137 to¦21137 them¦21138,
\v 30 “\add This\add* man¦21142, the¦21142 master¦21143 of¦21143 \add Egypt\add*, spoke¦21141 harshly¦21146 to us and¦21147 treated us¦21148 as¦21149 if we were spying¦21149 out¦21149 \add his country\add*,
\v 31 but¦21154 we¦21154 told¦21154 him¦21155, ‘We're honest¦21156—we're¦21159 not¦21158 spies¦21160.
\v 32 We're twelve brothers¦21166, the sons¦21167 of¦21167 our¦21168 father¦21168. One¦21169 is¦21170 \add no longer\add* alive, and the¦21171 youngest¦21171 is¦21173 with¦21173 our father today \add back\add* in¦21176 Canaan¦21177.’
\v 33 Then¦21179 \add that\add* man¦21181 told¦21179 us¦21180, ‘\add I'll find out if you're honest\add*: Leave¦21191 one¦21190 of¦21195 your brothers¦21189 with¦21184 me¦21192, and¦21193 take¦21197 grain for your families¦21196 and¦21198 go¦21198.
\v 34 Then bring¦21200 me¦21205 your youngest brother so¦21206 that¦21206 I'll know¦21206 you're not¦21208 spies¦21209—that you're \add telling the truth\add*. Then I'll \add release\add* your brother and¦21200 you can trade \add here freely\add*.’ ”
\p
\v 35 Then¦21224 \add when\add* they¦21225 \add started\add* emptying¦21226 their sacks¦21227, wow, each¦21230 man's¦21230 pouch of¦21231 silver¦21233 was¦21234 inside his¦21234 sack¦21234. \add When the brothers\add* and¦21224 their¦21227 father¦21241 saw¦21235 their¦21239 pouches of silver, they were¦21242 afraid¦21242.
\v 36 “You've \add deprived\add* me \add of another son\add*,” Yacob¦21246 \add exclaimed\add*. Yosef¦21250 is¦21251 \add gone\add*! Simeon's¦21252 \add gone\add*! And¦21252 \add then you want to take\add* Benyamin¦21256 \add as well\add*! \add Why's everything going so wrong for\add* me¦21258?”
\v 37 Then¦21262 Reuben¦21263 \add offered\add* to¦21264 his¦21266 father¦21266, “You¦21272 can kill¦21272 my¦21282 two¦21270 sons¦21271 if¦21273 I¦21283 don't¦21275 bring¦21276 \add Benyamin back\add* to you. \add Let me take responsibility for him\add*, and¦21262 I'll¦21276 \add make sure that he\add* returns¦21284 to¦21285 you¦21285.”
\v 38 “My¦21291 son¦21291 will¦21290 \em not\em* go¦21290 with¦21302 you,” Yacob¦21312 countered, “because¦21293 his¦21295 brother¦21295 is dead, and¦21287 he¦21287 alone¦21298 is¦21299 left. And¦21300 if¦21293 harm¦21301 were to meet him¦21300 on¦21302 the road that¦21303 you're going on, then¦21287 you'd bring¦21307 my¦21310 grey¦21310 head down¦21290 to¦21300 the grave in¦21302 grief.”
\c 43
\s1 Yosef's brothers bring Benyamin to Egypt
\rem /s1 Joseph's Brothers Return to Egypt with Benjamin; The Brothers Come Again, Bringing Benjamin; The Brothers Return to Egypt; The Second Journey to Egypt
\p
\v 1 \add However,\add* the¦21314 famine¦21314 in the region was¦21315 \add still\add* very severe,
\v 2 \add so\add* when they'd \add used up all\add* the¦21324 grain¦21324 that¦21325 they'd brought¦21326 from¦21327 Egypt¦21327, their¦21330 father¦21330 \add instructed\add* them¦21329, “Go¦21322 back¦21331 \add there and\add* buy¦21332 \add some\add* food¦21337 for¦21334 us¦21334.”
\p
\v 3 \add But\add* Yehudah¦21341 \add challenged\add* him¦21340, “\add That\add* man¦21346 \add ≈told¦21339 us that he¦21344 won't¦21348 even see¦21350 us\add* unless \add our\add* brother¦21353 is¦21354 with¦21345 \add us\add*.
\v 4 We¦21360 can \add only\add* go¦21364 \add there\add* to buy¦21365 food¦21367 for¦21366 you¦21358 if you'll \add agree to\add* send¦21359 our¦21362 \add youngest\add* brother¦21362 with¦21363 us¦21363.
\v 5 If¦21375 you¦21371 don't¦21371, \add it's not even worth going, because that\add* man¦21377 \add clearly told us that he won't even see us\add* unless \add our\add* brother¦21385 is¦21386 with¦21386 \add us\add*.
\p
\v 6 “Why¦21390 did¦21391 you all treat me¦21392 badly by telling¦21388 the man¦21394 you have \add a younger\add* brother¦21397?” \add their father\add* Yisra'el¦21389 asked¦21388.
\p
\v 7 “The¦21403 man¦21403 \add kept asking\add* about¦21405 us¦21404 and¦21399 our¦21405 relatives¦21405,” they¦21399 answered¦21399. “\add He asked\add*, ‘Is¦21408 your father¦21408 still¦21407 alive¦21409? Do¦21423 you have another brother?’ \add We just answered what he asked\add*. How could we possibly¦21422 know¦21423 that¦21424 he¦21425 would¦21425 \add insist that we bring our youngest brother there\add*?”
\p
\v 8 Then¦21431 Yehudah¦21432 said¦21431 to¦21433 his¦21436 father¦21436, “\add Entrust\add* the¦21438 young¦21438 man¦21438 to me¦21439, and¦21440 we'll¦21440 \add pack\add* up and¦21441 go¦21441 so¦21442 that¦21442 both¦21445 we¦21447 and you¦21450 and our¦21453 little¦21453 ones will¦21441 live¦21442 and¦21443 not¦21443 die¦21444.
\v 9 I myself will¦21456 guarantee him. \add If anything happens to him, you can charge it to me\add*. If¦21459 I don't¦21461 \add get\add* him \add safely\add* back to you, then I'll¦21456 \add personally\add* carry the blame¦21466 \add for the rest of my life\add*.
\v 10 \add If we hadn't sat around here doing nothing for so long\add*, certainly by now¦21477 we¦21478 could have¦21478 \add been there and back\add* twice!”
